\subsection{C3: Certified Clock Profiling}
\label{subsec:res_c3}
Table~\ref{tab:c3_control} isolates Phase~II by giving four configurations
the same initializers. Exactness comes first: the clock coordinate is
removed without altering the optimization problem, so the profiled
three-dimensional route should reach what the well-scaled explicit
four-dimensional route reaches. It does. Conditional position and clock RMSE
agree to every reported digit, and trial by trial the two solutions differ
by at most $0.39~\mu$m in position over the inliers --- the tolerance of the
outer optimizer, not a price paid for having eliminated a coordinate. This is the empirical face of the exact nesting of
Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}(i), and it is what licenses reading the remaining
columns as free.

The reduction also takes the clock unit out of the problem, because the
unit fixes the relative scaling of a coordinate the optimizer must trust.
Carried in seconds rather than range units, the explicit search degrades
conditional position RMSE by $32\%$; CCOP-JVP returns bit-identical output
under all three labels, as Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}(ii) requires. An accuracy
quoted for the explicit route is therefore meaningful only together with its
parameterization; the profiled route carries no such qualifier.

Every conditional clock solve is certified rather than merely converged.
Across the $100$ trials of Table~\ref{tab:c3_control} all $950$ clock
profiles returned a gap below tolerance, the worst at $0.998$ of it, the
subdivision cap never being reached; a larger independently seeded audit is
supplementary. This is what the finite trigonometric polynomial of
Section~\ref{subsec:ccop} buys: because the conditional clock dependence is
a polynomial of known degree, an FFT candidate set and branch-and-bound
refinement make the inner one-dimensional maximization global to a
deterministic tolerance at every visited position
(Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}(iii)), so any Phase~II failure is attributable to
the outer three-dimensional search alone. It certifies the inner problem, not
that outer search.

The dimension reduction follows from the same structure rather than from
tuning: because the clock is profiled exactly, the outer problem is
three-dimensional by construction, and the median evaluation count falls from
$13$ to $9$. Exactness, unit invariance, a conditional certificate and a
smaller outer search are one consequence of the trigonometric factorization,
not four independent claims.

