\subsection{Robustness, Complexity and Deployment Limits}
\label{subsec:res_robustness}
Three sensitivities set the deployment envelope, two of them in
Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}; the model-order panel, the other mismatch and
generalization sweeps and the Phase~I constant audit are supplementary.

Calibration is the binding one
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(a)--(b)): RIS--BS angular error degrades
precision long before capture,
raising conditional RMSE from $0.416$ to $31.1$~mm at $0.25^\circ$ while
the basin rate holds at $99.58\%$, and capture itself collapses only at
$1^\circ$ ($58.54\%$). Receiver gain, phase and sensor-position mismatch,
RIS element-phase error and first-order colored noise are all milder over
their tested ranges.

Model order is a material assumption rather than a tuning
parameter. In the dedicated $0$-dB sweep, $\widehat K=2$ is structurally
destructive ($0.705$-m median, $P_{\rm cat}=65.6\%$), and $\widehat K=4$
and $5$ keep submillimeter medians while carrying catastrophic rates above
a third.

Delay coincidence bounds acquisition where information does not
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(c)). Across a $5\times5\times2$ position grid the proposed median per-position
catastrophic rate is $0.62\%$ against $2.71\%$ for 4D-JVP, and both hard
corners lie near a cascaded-delay coincidence surface, the worst reaching
$29.58\%$ at a minimum separation of $0.062/B_{\rm occ}$ -- while the
free-Jones EFIM stays full rank at every tested position, corners included.
Local information sufficiency therefore does not imply global acquisition
reliability: screening needs a delay-coincidence check and a coarse global
fallback, not a PEB map alone. Every sweep holds one infrastructure layout
and samples no panel-placement diversity.

\label{subsec:res_cost}
On one host at $-10$~dB the proposed route requires $3.18$~s and $2.52$~GB
on the $96$ receive chains a full EVS costs, between 4D-JVP ($2.40$~s) and
Algebraic CPD ($4.38$~s). Of that total $2.83$~s falls in Phase~I against
$0.34$~s in certified clock profiling, so acquisition dominates the budget --
the same stage the reliability gain comes from. Breakdowns are
supplementary.

A $25\%$ cyclic prefix at $\Delta f=5$~MHz is $50$~ns, covering the
$19.1$-ns cascaded-delay spread plus clock-search interval, so one
configuration per symbol gives $T_{\rm train}\simeq64~\mu$s at $T=256$ and
the single-coherence-block assumption of Section~\ref{sec:system_model}
holds; the UE-displacement check is supplementary.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied joint 3-D localization and clock synchronization of a
single-antenna user in a blocked-LoS distributed near-field RIS uplink
observed by one shared electromagnetic vector-sensor array. The problem is
organized by a single algebraic fact -- the per-panel sensing block factors
into a space--frequency term and a calibrated Maxwell--Jones term -- which
fixes at once how much information a given receiver mode retains, whether that
mode still keeps panels separable, how far the data can be compressed without
loss, and how the common clock is profiled to a certified inner optimum in a
form that does not depend on the unit the clock is carried in. On
this structure we built a two-phase estimator that acquires and associates the
cascaded factors inside the sufficient subspace, then refines position and
clock with the Jones coefficients eliminated analytically.

Simulations place the resulting gain in acquisition reliability rather than in
local precision: the proposed route matches the closest in-model control in
precision once both reach the correct basin, but incurs a substantially lower
catastrophic rate. The broader lesson is that a local information bound does not
characterize that reliability -- the weight a reduced receiver costs is
panel- and polarization-dependent, so it can leave one panel almost
unobserved, and the free-Jones information matrix stays full rank even where
acquisition is least reliable. Receiver-mode selection and panel deployment
should therefore be screened by Maxwell-subspace and cascaded-delay
separation, not by a bound map alone. Within the calibrated, known-panel-count
model, the most direct opening is a polynomial-time association surrogate for
larger panel counts.

\balance
